The audience has to know this world from everyday life, well enough to reason inside it without
explanation. Use the part of it they have lived or watched, not a specialist corner.

Copy the shape, not the surface. Give every part of the skeleton a counterpart: each player, the
constraint, the tradeoff, the cost of doing nothing, and the decision. Run the causal chain the
same way, link for link, so a listener who reasons inside the analogy reaches the conclusion the
real situation calls for.

The spoken version is two or three sentences the speaker can say without notes. It carries the
spine: what keeps happening, where that leads, and the choice. A counterpart that does not fit in
those sentences still belongs in the mappings. The decision line is the part of the spoken
version that points at the choice. The mappings say what stands for what. The breaking point
names where the comparison stops matching reality. Every analogy breaks somewhere, and naming the
place honestly lets the speaker get there before a skeptic does.
